```python
@dataclass
class LayerScatterModes:
    layer_input_mode: ScatterMode
    attn_mode: ScatterMode
    # Can be further split into e.g. mlp_input_mode and mlp_output_mode if needed
    mlp_mode: ScatterMode
    middle_residual_mode: ScatterMode
    layer_output_mode: ScatterMode

    @classmethod
    def init_new(cls, **kwargs):
        context = _LayerModeComputationContext(**kwargs)
        return cls(
            layer_input_mode=cls._compute_layer_input_mode(context),
            attn_mode=ScatterMode.TP_ATTN_FULL,
            mlp_mode=cls._compute_mlp_mode(context),
            middle_residual_mode=cls._compute_middle_residual_mode(context),
            layer_output_mode=cls._compute_layer_output_mode(context),
        )

    @classmethod
    def _compute_layer_input_mode(cls, context: _LayerModeComputationContext):
        if context.layer_id == 0:
            return ScatterMode.model_input_output()
        return cls._compute_layer_output_mode(context.previous_layer())

    @classmethod
    def _compute_mlp_mode(cls, context: _LayerModeComputationContext):
        if context.is_layer_sparse:
            return (
                ScatterMode.SCATTERED
                if (
                    # Token dispatch/combine will be handled outside of LayerCommunicator for these modes.
                    not get_moe_a2a_backend().is_none()
                    or should_use_flashinfer_cutlass_moe_fp4_allgather()
                )
                else ScatterMode.FULL
            )
        else:
            return (
                ScatterMode.SCATTERED
                if enable_moe_dense_fully_dp()
                else ScatterMode.FULL
            )

    @classmethod
    def _should_gather_for_tbo(cls, context: _LayerModeComputationContext):
        return (
            not context.is_layer_sparse
            and context.is_next_layer_sparse
            and enable_moe_dense_fully_dp()
            and get_global_server_args().enable_two_batch_overlap
        )

    @classmethod
    def _compute_middle_residual_mode(cls, context: _LayerModeComputationContext):
        mlp_mode = cls._compute_mlp_mode(context)
        if mlp_mode == ScatterMode.SCATTERED:
            return ScatterMode.SCATTERED
        if mlp_mode == ScatterMode.FULL:
            return ScatterMode.TP_ATTN_FULL
        raise NotImplementedError

    @classmethod
    def _compute_layer_output_mode(cls, context: _LayerModeComputationContext):
        mlp_mode = cls._compute_mlp_mode(context)
        if context.layer_id == context.num_layers - 1:
            return ScatterMode.model_input_output()
        if mlp_mode == ScatterMode.SCATTERED:
            if cls._should_gather_for_tbo(context):
                return ScatterMode.TP_ATTN_FULL
            return ScatterMode.SCATTERED
        if mlp_mode == ScatterMode.FULL:
            return ScatterMode.TP_ATTN_FULL
        raise NotImplementedError


def enable_moe_dense_fully_dp():
    return get_global_server_args().moe_dense_tp_size == 1
```
</details>

Furthermore, even though `DeepseekV2AttentionMLA`/`DeepseekV2MoE`/`DeepseekV2MLP` objects have to be consistent with their surrounding communications, the results of these decisions trees are not fed into them and vice versa. For example, if MoE is TP, then the inputs have to be full tensors instead of scattered tensors. Instead of querying `LayerScatterModes`, `DeepseekV2MoE` looks at the global config
```python
class DeepseekV2MoE(nn.Module):

    def __init__(
        self,
        config: PretrainedConfig,
        layer_id: int,
        quant_config: Optional[QuantizationConfig] = None,
        prefix: str = "",
        alt_stream: Optional[torch.cuda.Stream] = None,
        is_nextn: bool = False,
    ):
        super().__init__()
        self.tp_size = get_tensor_model_parallel_world_size()
        self.moe_ep_size = get_moe_expert_parallel_world_size()
```

Together, these make it extremely difficult to reason about the consistency, and therefore correctness, of the communication kernels.

At a high level, the current architecture is attempting to build a consistent model while making local decisions scattered everywhere. There is no enforcement that each decision is consistent with each other; they have to be checked manually. I believe there is a better way: track how the activation/residuals are being sharded through the model forward pass and enforce consistency.

# Proposal
Rewrite the communications such that they're decided in `DeepseekV2Model`, then injected into each individual `DeepseekV2DecoderLayer`. In pseudocode, it is
```python
def compute_selected_ops(config, allow_allreduce_fusion: bool):
    # Based on model and server config
    layer_transforms = make_layer_transforms(config, allow_allreduce_fusion)

    if is_using_dp(config):
        hidden_states = ShardingMode.SplitToken
        residual = ShardingMode.SplitToken
    else
        hidden_states = ShardingMode.Full
        residual = ShardingMode.Full

    selected_ops = []
    for layer in layers:
        hidden_states, residual, selected_op = layer.select(hidden_states, residual)
        selected_ops.append(selected_op)

    return selected_ops

class DeepseekV2Model(nn.Module):
    def __init__(
        #...
    ):
        # ...
        selected_ops_no_fusion = compute_selected_ops(config, allow_allreduce_fusion=False)
        selected_ops_allreduce_fusion = compute_selected_ops(config, allow_allreduce_fusion=True)
        def selected_ops(num_tokens: int):
            if num_tokens > FUSE_ALLREDUCE_MAX_BATCH_SIZE:
                return selected_ops_no_fusion
            else:
                return selected_ops_allreduce_fusion

        self.layers, self.start_layer, self.end_layer = make_layers(
            config.num_hidden_layers,
            lambda idx, prefix: DeepseekV2DecoderLayer(
                selected_ops,
                # ...
            ),
```
Notably, `DeepseekV2DecoderLayer` should depend entirely on `selected_ops` and not make its own decisions.
An example of what `layer_transforms` might look like is
```python    
class LayerTransform:
    pass

class DenseLayerTransform(LayerTransform):
    def select(self, hidden_states: ShardingMode, residual: ShardingMode) -> tuple[ShardingMode, ShardingMode, list[Ops]]:
        hidden_states, residual, attn_ops = self.select_attn(hidden_states, residual)
        hidden_states, residual, mlp_ops = self.select_mlp(hidden_states, residual)
        hidden_states, residual, post_process_op = # ...

        return hidden_states, residual, [*attn_ops, *mlp_ops, post_process_op]

    def select_attn(self, hidden_states: ShardingMode, residual: ShardingMode) -> tuple[ShardingMode, ShardingMode, list[Ops]]:
        if hidden_states == ShardingMode.Full:
            prepare_attn_op = PrepareAttentionOp.no_communication()

            attn_op = AttentionOp( #...
                num_local_heads=config.num_attention_heads // self.attn_tp_size,  # enforce consistency with communication
            )

            if self.attn_uses_tp():
                hidden_states = ShardingMode.Partial
        elif hidden_states == ShardingMode.SplitToken:
            prepare_attn_op = PrepareAttentionOp.gather_to_tp_group()
            # ...
        elif hidden_states == ShardingMode.Partial:
            prepare_attn_op = PrepareAttentionOp.reduce_scatter_to_tp_group()

        # ...

        return hidden_states, residual, [prepare_attn_op, attn_op]

    def select_mlp(self, hidden_states: ShardingMode, residual: ShardingMode) -> tuple[ShardingMode, ShardingMode, list[Ops]]:
        if hidden_states == ShardingMode.Full: 
            prepare_mlp_op = PrepareMLPOp.no_communication()
            mlp_op = MLPOp(
                self.mlp_tp_size,  # enforce consistency with communication
                # ...
            )

            if self.mlp_uses_tp():
                hidden_states = ShardingMode.Partial
        elif hidden_states == ShardingMode.Partial:
            prepare_mlp_op = PrepareMLPOp.reduce_scatter()
            mlp_op = MLPOp(
                # ...
            )
            hidden_states = ShardingMode.SplitToken

        # ...

        return hidden_states, residual, [prepare_mlp_op, mlp_op]
```
With a finite state machine design, the correctness of each transformation should be easier to check, e.g.,
performing allreduce on `hidden_states` with `ShardingMode.SplitToken` in #18232 should be easy to spot.
Since we go through the entire forward pass, this design enforces the consistency of the model end-to-end.

# `torch.DTensor`
`torch.DTensor` is torch's native solution to distributed tensors. [An example](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/torch/distributed/tensor/README.md#compiler-and-pytorch-dtensor) from torch is
```python
import torch
from torch.distributed.tensor import DTensor, Shard, Replicate, distribute_tensor, distribute_module, init_device_mesh

# construct a device mesh with available devices (multi-host or single host)
device_mesh = init_device_mesh("cuda", (4,))
# if we want to do row-wise sharding
rowwise_placement=[Shard(0)]
# if we want to do col-wise sharding
colwise_placement=[Shard(1)]

big_tensor = torch.randn(888, 12)
# distributed tensor returned will be sharded across the dimension specified in placements
rowwise_tensor = distribute_tensor(big_tensor, device_mesh=device_mesh, placements=rowwise_placement)
# reshard the current row-wise tensor to a colwise tensor or replicate tensor
colwise_tensor = rowwise_tensor.redistribute(device_mesh, colwise_placement)
```
Likewise, it tracks how the DTensor is being sharded and enforces correct communications. With DTensor-aware torch ops, a distributed module can be implemented as
```python
def shard_params(mod, device_mesh):
    col_linear_placement = [Shard(0)]
    # shard fc1 and fc2
    if isinstance(mod, nn.Linear):
        for name, param in mod.named_parameters():
            dist_param = nn.Parameter(
                distribute_tensor(param, device_mesh, colwise_placement)
            )
            mod.register_parameter(name, dist_param)

sharded_module = distribute_module(MyModule(), device_mesh, partition_fn=shard_params)
```

DTensor is is a high-level abstraction, aiming at ease-of-use. DTensor's primary objective is to abstract away the existence of communication kernels, and consequently, DTensor has several problems for sglang.
1. There is a global registry to overwrite communication kernels, but there is no way to control the communication kernel at each call-site.
2. It requires rewriting *every* torch custom op to be DTensor-aware. While shims can be created to convert DTensors to and from Tensors for each custom op, it defeats the point of DTensors.
3. There is a predefined list of communication types allowed. There is no such thing as fused communication kernels, e.g., flashinfer all-reduce fusion.
4. All communications are synchronous w.r.t. other kernels. There can be no communication/computation overlap, e.g., single batch overlap.

Point 2 will take significant engineering effort. Points 3 and 4 are likely insurmountable and renders DTensor infeasible.

## Problem
Adding support for and achieving feature parity with existing hardware platforms in sglang requires significant effort especially if it needs to be done out of tree, currently hardware dependent logic is tightly intertwined with the sglang runtime as we se `is_[hardware_platform]()` checks and conditional branches all over the place which not only makes adding new things and code hard to maintaining but enforces in tree integration of hardware platforms.

Well a solution to this kinda already exists in the runtime in multiple forms: platform plugins, graph runners, operator runners like FP8 GEMM and MOE RUNNER but they are not cohesively or consistently used, there is no well defined demarcation in the engine that acts as a boundary between device independent and device dependent code. Triton kernels being all over the place also further complicates this for platforms that have none or partial triton support as they are required to do monkey patching due to the lack of extensibility.

Bigger components for example Flash Attention backend should be re-useable as long as the under lying platform supports the flash attention api

There is already an abstraction over MOE RUNNER and FP8 GEMM RUNNER for different implementations (e.g flashinfer, deepgemm, trtllm) but no way for platform plugins to provide there own implementations

The hooks system provides a nice way to essentially override anything (the way I see, it hides explicit monkey patching) but we should strive towards providing extensible runtime base classes and to ensure hardware platform vendors have the right amount of control over the execution to maximise performance while minimizing the amount of custom code they need to maintain
## Solution
It all starts with formalizing the platform plugin system and extensively adopting it throughout the runtime, i.e getting rid of all `is_[hardware_platform]()` checks and ensure they flow through the platform class.

Around the platform class we hope to establish a harware abstraction layer (referred as sgl-hal). 
### Device dependent logic

a low level component of handling device dependent logic is the `MultiPlatformDispatcher` exposes the capabilities of the `MultiPlatformOp` without being dependent on `torch.nn.Module`. `MultiPlatformOp` is not changed from a public api point of view ensuring existing logic doesn't break

The goal is to not directly import sgl-kernel or any other platform dependent modules, they should be wrapped in a dispatcher and consumed where device dependent code is import lazily as part of init, this will apply to attention backends, triton kernels, deepgeem and flashinfer usage etc.

```python
class _MultiPlatformBase:
    # OOT forward registry: maps dispatch_key -> {op_cls -> forward_fn}
    _oot_forward_registry: ClassVar[dict[str, dict[type, Callable]]] = {}

    @classmethod
    def register_oot_forward(cls, op_cls: type, fn: Callable, platform_key: str):
        """Register an OOT forward implementation for a specific op class and platform."""
        cls._oot_forward_registry.setdefault(platform_key, {})[op_cls] = fn

    def forward_native(self, *args, **kwargs):
        raise NotImplementedError

    def forward_cuda(self, *args, **kwargs):
        raise NotImplementedError

    def forward_npu(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_native(*args, **kwargs)

    def forward_hip(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_cuda(*args, **kwargs)

    def forward_xpu(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_native(*args, **kwargs)

    def forward_musa(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_cuda(*args, **kwargs)

    def forward_hpu(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_native(*args, **kwargs)

    def forward_cpu(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self.forward_native(*args, **kwargs)

    def dispatch_forward(self):
        # OOT platform dispatch: check registry then method lookup
        if current_platform.is_out_of_tree():
            key = current_platform.get_dispatch_key_name()
            oot = self._oot_forward_registry.get(key, {})
            if type(self) in oot:
                return oot[type(self)].__get__(self)
            method = getattr(self, f"forward_{key}", None)
            if method is not None:
                return method
            return self.forward_native

        if _is_cuda:
            return self.forward_cuda
        elif _is_hip:
            return self.forward_hip
        elif _is_cpu and _is_cpu_amx_available:
            return self.forward_cpu
        elif _is_npu:
            return self.forward_npu
        elif _is_xpu:
            return self.forward_xpu
        elif _is_musa:
            return self.forward_musa
        else:
            return self.forward_native


class MultiPlatformDispatcher(_MultiPlatformBase):
    def __init__(self):
        self._forward_method: Callable = self.dispatch_forward()

    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self._forward_method(*args, **kwargs)


class MultiPlatformOp(nn.Module, _MultiPlatformBase):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self._forward_method: Callable = self.dispatch_forward()

        # States for torch.compile
        self._original_forward_method = None
        self.is_torch_compile = False

    def enter_torch_compile(self, num_tokens: int):
        # Skip if Op is already entered compile mode.
        # NOTE(alcanderian): Some Ops(for example RotaryEmbedding) will be reused
        # among layers and `enter_torch_compile` will be called many times.
        # We should prevent `self._original_forward_method` from being overridden when
        # it is not the first time `enter_torch_compile` called.
        if self.is_torch_compile:
            return

        self._original_forward_method = self._forward_method
        # NOTE: Temporarily workaround MoE
        # The performance of torch.compile on this layer is not always good when bs > 1,
        # so we decide to only use torch.compile when bs=1
        if "FusedMoE" in self.__class__.__name__:
            if num_tokens == 1:
                from sglang.srt.layers.moe.fused_moe_native import (
                    fused_moe_forward_native,
                )

                self._forward_method = fused_moe_forward_native
        elif "TopK" in self.__class__.__name__:
            if num_tokens == 1:
                self._forward_method = self.forward_native
        else:
            self._forward_method = self.forward_native
        self.is_torch_compile = True

    def leave_torch_compile(self):
        # Skip if Op is already exited compile mode.
        if not self.is_torch_compile:
            return

        self._forward_method = self._original_forward_method
        self._original_forward_method = None
        self.is_torch_compile = False

    # Please do not override this method, because `self._forward_method` can change when in torch compile mode
    @debug_kernel_api
    def forward(self, *args, **kwargs):
        return self._forward_method(*args, **kwargs)
```

### Example: Hardware Independent Flash Attention Backend
This is a minimal example showcasing how the existing flash attention backend can be functionally hardware independent as long as the underlying HW implements the `flash_attn_varlen_func` and `flash_attn_with_kvcache` APIs, the actual backend initializes the classes bellow and invokes them as normal when required
```python
class FlashAttnVarlenFuncOp(MultiPlatformDispatcher):

    def __init__(self, fa_impl_ver: int = 3) -> None:
        super().__init__()
        self.fa_impl_ver = fa_impl_ver

    def _resolve_impl(self):
        if self.fa_impl_ver == 3:
            from sgl_kernel.flash_attn import flash_attn_varlen_func
        elif self.fa_impl_ver == 4:
            from sglang.jit_kernel.flash_attention_v4 import flash_attn_varlen_func
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid fa_impl_ver: {self.fa_impl_ver}")
        return flash_attn_varlen_func

    def forward_native(
		...
    ):
        return self._resolve_impl()(
			...
        )

    def forward_cuda(
		...
    ):
        return self._resolve_impl()(
			...
        )


class FlashAttnWithKvcacheOp(MultiPlatformDispatcher):

    def __init__(self, fa_impl_ver: int = 3) -> None:
        super().__init__()
        self.fa_impl_ver = fa_impl_ver

    def _resolve_impl(self):
        if self.fa_impl_ver == 3:
            from sgl_kernel.flash_attn import flash_attn_with_kvcache
        elif self.fa_impl_ver == 4:
            from sglang.jit_kernel.flash_attention_v4 import flash_attn_with_kvcache
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid fa_impl_ver: {self.fa_impl_ver}")
        return flash_attn_with_kvcache

    def forward_native(
		...
    ):
        return self._resolve_impl()(
			...
        )

    def forward_cuda(
		...
    ):
        return self._resolve_impl()(
			...
        )
```

### Example: BMM Handling in Deepseek
All across the runtime there are checks for things which are supported by a set of platforms but not others, as an example the following in deepseek's forward_mla where based on the platform the bmm is performed in fp8 or bf16
```python
if _is_cpu:
	q_nope_out = torch.bmm(
		q_nope.to(torch.bfloat16).transpose(0, 1),
		self.w_kc.to(torch.bfloat16) * self.w_scale,
	)
else:
	# fix bmm_fp8 error under cublas12.9 caused by bumpallocator, detail in pr#11612
	q_nope_val, q_nope_scale = per_tensor_quant_mla_fp8(
		q_nope.transpose(0, 1),
		(
			torch.zeros((1,), dtype=torch.float32, device=q_nope.device)
			if _is_cublas_ge_129
			else zero_allocator.allocate(1)
		),
	)
	q_nope_out = bmm_fp8(
		q_nope_val, self.w_kc, q_nope_scale, self.w_scale, torch.bfloat16
	)
```
By default the above logic for OOT plugins will go to the else block which would try to invoke a triton kernel and `bmm_fp8` from sgl-kernel either of them may not be supported on the OOT platform, this also is an example for triton usage being spread all across the runtime. the hook system cannot easily help here as the above if else block is part of a bigger function

Device Dispatcher can be re-used for this case too, in general device dispatcher can be used to encapsulate any device dependent logic and platform plugins can register there own handlers
```python
class BmmDispatcher(MultiPlatformDispatcher):
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        super().__init__()

    def forward_cpu(self, q_nope, w_kc, w_scale):
	    return torch.bmm(
			q_nope.to(torch.bfloat16).transpose(0, 1),
			self.w_kc.to(torch.bfloat16) * self.w_scale,
		)

	def forward_cuda(self, q_nope, w_kc, w_scale):
		from sgl_kernel import bmm_fp8

		q_nope_val, q_nope_scale = per_tensor_quant_mla_fp8(
			q_nope.transpose(0, 1),
			(
				torch.zeros((1,), dtype=torch.float32, device=q_nope.device)
				if _is_cublas_ge_129
				else zero_allocator.allocate(1)
			),
		)
		return bmm_fp8(
			q_nope_val, self.w_kc, q_nope_scale, self.w_scale, torch.bfloat16
		)
```

### Runtime components
Device dispatcher provide control at the lowest level to the platform plugins and the is the path for easiest enablement of sglang on new hardware platforms. For better control, to achieve best possible performance hardware platforms will opt into taking full control over some components, current platform plugins system already allows for this but will be expanded over time as per requests from plugin developers, for example as one of the first extensions we want to expose the quant configs to the plugin system so that HW vendors can provide there own implementation of quant schemes, as a continuing effort we hope to refactor the current components and expose API that can be overriden preserving control over device independent code

